          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is value-based bidding in Google Ads?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Value-based bidding is a smart bidding approach where Google optimises to the conversion value you report rather than to a flat cost per conversion. The platform predicts the value of each potential click and bids more for the ones likely to be worth more. It is only as good as the value you feed it: report revenue and it chases revenue, report profit and it chases profit." }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I send revenue or profit as my conversion value?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Send profit where you can. If you report order revenue, Google treats a low-margin sale and a high-margin sale of the same price as equally valuable, so it will happily chase cheap, low-margin volume. Passing gross profit as the conversion value tells the algorithm that a high-margin order is worth more, and it bids accordingly. Revenue-based values are a reasonable starting point only when margins are broadly similar across the catalogue." }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I calculate a profit-based conversion value?",
          "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Take the order value and subtract cost of goods, payment fees, shipping and expected returns to get contribution profit. The simplest implementation is a margin multiplier: if a product group runs at a 40 percent margin, report 40 percent of order value as the conversion value. Where you have per-order or per-product cost data, pass the actual profit figure through offline or enhanced conversions for greater accuracy." }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What target ROAS should I pair with profit values?",
